     1	# Protocol framework — design (v7, 2026-09-28)
     2	
     3	_Status: v7 DRAFT. v6 answered the second Astra audit; v7 answers the
     4	third (`docs/audit/framework-v6-audit-report.md`: REVISE, 3 blocking,
     5	6 major, 0 minor; the framework items W01, W02, W04, W06, W08, W09). The exact FG
     6	statements moved to `docs/FG-COMPOSITION.md`, a separate versioned
     7	registry that follows the research. This file fixes the framework and
     8	the FG interface shapes only. The user APPROVED §0–§6 and §9 on
     9	2026-09-28; §7 stays DRAFT until Phase 4._
    10	
    11	_History: v0–v2 core design agreed by both sessions. v3–v4 user FG
    12	decisions (fixed Simplex FG, anchors, modular recovery, HavocFG). v5
    13	answers the first Astra audit. v6 answers the second; §11 maps the
    14	findings._
    15	
    16	## 0. Purpose and scope
    17	
    18	The current repository proves one fixed bundle (`AvailableChain`,
    19	Draft 4.3) per protocol. This design replaces the bundle with a framework
    20	that stays stable while the protocol search changes direction.
    21	
    22	```text
    23	┌────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    24	│ G1 │ Protocol-agnostic within the engine scope (below).                │
    25	│ G2 │ Additive evolution: a new assumption, claim, output kind or key   │
    26	│    │ scheme is an addition; existing certificates stay valid.          │
    27	│ G3 │ Partial certification: an instance certifies any subset of the    │
    28	│    │ claims, at any number of parameter points, over one fixed         │
    29	│    │ protocol and facet bundle.                                        │
    30	│ G4 │ Mechanical comparison: checked transports and Pareto sets.        │
    31	│ G5 │ Composition with one fixed, verified FG (our Simplex FG) through   │
    32	│    │ a typed interface.                                                │
    33	│ G6 │ Cheap falsification: executable instances, kernel-checked         │
    34	│    │ refutations.                                                      │
    35	└────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    36	```
    37	
    38	Non-goals: probability inside Lean; one generic proof for all
    39	protocols; porting verified-consensus; unbounded ("eventually")
    40	liveness (every catalog claim has a deadline).
    41	
    42	**Engine scope.** Engine v2 covers scheduled-duty models: a finite
    43	validator set; labels with a fixed act time and seat count; committees
    44	or the full validator set, with public assignment conditions;
    45	identity-free honest code (no private key or private randomness); one
    46	global time; a static honest set; finite runs; emission only on ticks.
    47	
    48	Out of scope by decision (user, 2026-09-28): private VRF
    49	self-selection. Also outside v2, each needing a new engine version with a
    50	lift simulation: clock drift (one logical duty at different local
    51	times); signed replies on receipt; explicit compromise or key erasure;
    52	unbounded liveness.
    53	
    54	Additions inside the scope (not covered by existing certificates):
    55	integer stake weights (seat multiplicity, weighted links and quorum
    56	lemmas; the unit-weight FG proofs do not transfer by themselves); fixed
    57	size sampled committees with a Balanced-ε premise; public fixed-size age
    58	buckets; inclusion-list committees; multiple proposers with proposal
    59	identifiers and a selection claim; DAG state in scheduled models (the FG
    60	needs a chain projection); monotone corruption traces with new regimes;
    61	late joiners in a fixed validator set (after E6); protocol-driven relay
    62	with send provenance; signature aggregation with extraction proofs; a
    63	second FG as a new module with its own composition proofs (a gadget whose
    64	private record controls AP proposals needs a new isolation interface).
    65	
    66	## 1. Layers
    67	
    68	```text
    69	┌────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┐
    70	│ L  │ Layer        │ Content                       │ Change policy      │
    71	├────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
    72	│ L0 │ Engine       │ events, world, run, emission, │ versioned; content │
    73	│    │              │ restart, own-record step      │ hash frozen        │
    74	│ L1 │ Conditions   │ run predicates; regimes;      │ append-only        │
    75	│    │              │ execution-prefix predicates   │                    │
    76	│ LM │ Modules      │ fixed-component definitions:  │ versioned; content │
    77	│    │              │ Simplex FG, duty frames,      │ hash frozen        │
    78	│    │              │ policy type, AP template      │                    │
    79	│ L2 │ Facets       │ what an instance exposes      │ append-only        │
    80	│ L3 │ Catalog      │ claim specs, transports,      │ append-only;       │
    81	│    │              │ relation lemmas               │ immutable ids      │
    82	│ L4 │ Instances    │ protocol + parameters +       │ free               │
    83	│    │              │ facets; certificates and      │                    │
    84	│    │              │ refutations                   │                    │
    85	│ L5 │ Kits         │ proofs: engine, Goldfish, FG, │ free; never        │
    86	│    │              │ compose                       │ imported by L0–L3  │
    87	│ L6 │ Registry     │ generated matrix; explorer    │ generated          │
    88	└────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┘
    89	```
    90	
    91	L0, L1, LM, L2 and L3 import nothing from L4–L6. LM holds definitions
    92	only. A hole in L1–L3 is fixed by a new versioned entry plus, when
    93	useful, a relation lemma to the old one.
    94	
    95	## 2. Engine v2 (L0)
    96	
    97	**E1. Duties and keys.** `Auth = seat (ℓ : Label) (i : Fin (seats ℓ)) | none`.
    98	A proposal duty is a one-seat label. `act st ℓ` produces every duty
    99	payload. `ProtocolSpec` has no global `n`.
   100	
   101	**E2. Seats and assignment.** `seats : Label → Nat`,
   102	`Run.seatOf ℓ : Fin (seats ℓ) → V`. Committee models are L1 conditions:
   103	`CStar F` (seats = |V| and a proved `Fin (seats ℓ) ≃ V`), `Linked F` (one
   104	fixed bijection for all labels in F), `SingleSeat F`, and later
   105	`Weighted w F`, `Balanced ε F`. Honest code never sees keys. The
   106	protocol declares its AP label family `APLabels` and its FG label family
   107	explicitly (E5).
   108	
   109	**E3. Weights** are additions (seat multiplicity plus conditions).
   110	
   111	**E4. Delivery outcomes and deliverability (audit V01).**
   112	A delivery of `o` calls the handler; for each `o'` with `handles o o'`,
   113	the protocol classifies the call's effect on `o'` as one of:
   114	- *accepted*: `processed o'` becomes true;
   115	- *permanently rejected*: `excludes o'` holds from then on;
   116	- *temporarily refused*: the state does not change for `o'`, and
   117	  `excludes o'` is false (for example SA-PC's PC before
   118	  `pcEnvelopeReady`; a future-label faulty HEAD).
   119	
   120	A refusal that can never end in the current epoch is a PERMANENT
   121	rejection, and the protocol must report it through `excludes` (async
   122	review of v7). Examples: Grid CO's HEAD for label r after slot r+1 (the
   123	clock only goes forward; `Model/Protocol.lean:306`), and a HEAD from a
   124	seat that already has two HEADs at label r (a third equivocation).
   125	Today Grid CO sets `excludes := fun _ _ => false`, so Phase 1 must
   126	define it for these two cases, and for SA and SA-PC too.
   127	
   128	The network conditions are about handler calls, as today. Temporary
   129	refusal is allowed and needs no flag. Obligations (`HooksSound P`):
   130	- `excludes` is stable under `clock`, `process` and `recordOwn` within
   131	  an epoch, and implies that `process` leaves the state unchanged.
   132	  `restore` may clear an exclusion that depends on lost receipts (the
   133	  equivocation case), but not a time-based one;
   134	- `processed` and `depsPresent` are monotone WITHIN ONE RESTART EPOCH of
   135	  the node (`processed` may be derived; audit W04);
   136	- `handles o o'` implies `o = o' ∨ carries o o'`.
   137	
   138	Feasibility contract (audit W01). `HooksSound` alone does not give
   139	continuation: a protocol can emit a malformed object after one trigger,
   140	and then no synchronous run passes the next broadcast deadline. So every
   141	instance also proves:
   142	- `EmitWellFormed`: every object that an honest node emits satisfies
   143	  `wellFormed`;
   144	- `HandlerCoverage`: a delivery of `o` makes a handler call for `o` and
   145	  for every `o'` with `handles o o'`, as the outcome classification says;
   146	- `Deliverable`: every dependency of an honest emission was ACCEPTED by
   147	  the emitter before the emission (a faulty object is allowed), and the
   148	  emitter's acceptance order is a finite delivery plan;
   149	- `RelayFeasible`: if an honest node accepts `o` at t, then at an honest
   150	  receiver that has accepted `o`'s dependencies, a delivery of `o` before
   151	  t + Δ (after GST, or on a healthy prefix) is accepted or permanently
   152	  rejected (`excludes`), not temporarily refused.
   153	
   154	The acceptance-relay regime condition gets the same exemption as the
   155	handler-call relay in `PartialSynchrony`: no obligation for an object
   156	that the receiver excludes at the deadline. Today `AcceptedObjectRelay`
   157	and `HealthyAcceptedObjectRelay` (`Generic/Conditions.lean`) have NO
   158	exemption. So for Grid CO and SA, a faulty HEAD released to one honest
   159	node in the last Δ of its window takes the run out of EVERY regime:
   160	today's certificates do not cover that adversary behavior. This is a
   161	coverage gap in the current contract. Phase 1 fixes the condition and
   162	checks again each SA and SA-PC proof that uses `acceptedRelay` (it may
   163	use acceptance only before the object's exclusion time).
   164	
   165	`WindowMargin` (per instance, per object class; async review): the last
   166	cutoff at which a rule reads the object, plus Δ, is at most the
   167	receiver's exclusion time. With it, the exempt relay implies the old
   168	relay at every cutoff that the proofs use. Grid CO/SA: the label-r HEAD
   169	cutoffs are t_r+2Δ, +3Δ, +5Δ and +6Δ (`earlyTime`, `freezeTime`,
   170	`releaseTime`, `gateTime`); the window closes at t_r+8Δ; margin 2Δ ≥ Δ.
   171	An excluded third equivocation is harmless: the late test drops the
   172	seat on two accepted copies. FG rows: check the same margin for the
   173	w_F inclusion window in Phase 4.
   174	
   175	`Kit.Engine` proves the run-extension theorem from these four and
   176	`HooksSound`: every valid execution prefix in a regime extends by the
   177	canonical synchronous scheduler to any horizon, with both handler calls
   178	and acceptance relay. This gives continuation from every accepted input,
   179	including inputs that trigger malformed emissions (which the contract
   180	forbids).
   181	
   182	Two limits (async review):
   183	- Temporary refusal and synchrony. If the only delivery within Δ is
   184	  temporarily refused, the handler-call premise is met but the object is
   185	  not processed. A proof may assume later processing only if (a) the
   186	  regime includes the optional L1 condition `ReOffer` (the object is
   187	  delivered again within Δ after the first time it is no longer refused,
   188	  after GST), or (b) the protocol buffers the object in its state and
   189	  processes it later, or (c) the object reaches acceptance through the
   190	  relay premise (today's SA-PC). Each instance states which.
   191	- The run-extension theorem guarantees handler calls on the delivery
   192	  plan, not acceptance. A receiver may permanently reject an object that
   193	  the emitter accepted (for example FG admission with a different F,
   194	  possible only at a > f); excluded objects carry no obligation.
   195	
   196	These are NEW eligibility obligations. They are proved per migrated
   197	instance; they are not silently added to the v1 hooks (V07, M03).
   198	
   199	**E5. Own-signature record.**
   200	- `selfVisible : Label → Bool`, `recordOwn : State → Object → State`.
   201	- `World.step` takes the run context (seat assignment and `awake`).
   202	- Atomic tick: `clock`; every due payload from that post-clock state;
   203	  signing; `recordOwn` folded over self-visible objects in label order,
   204	  then seat order; release.
   205	- Separation: every label in `APLabels`, and every label in a `CStar`
   206	  family, has `selfVisible = false`.
   207	- Isolation laws, with `base : State → B`:
   208	  - `base` after `clock` and `process` is a function of `base` before and
   209	    of the time or object; `recordOwn` does not change `base`;
   210	  - `act` on `APLabels`, all gossip and every output getter factor
   211	    through `base`;
   212	  - restore law: `base (restore d)` depends only on the base-visible part
   213	    of `d` (for an FG composite: F and its basis), never on record-only
   214	    data (audit V11: otherwise a restart could copy a self-visible seat
   215	    index into the base);
   216	  - hook projection: `wellFormed`, `depsPresent`, `processed`,
   217	    `excludes` and `handles` restricted to AP objects factor through
   218	    `base`, so delivery regimes transport between a composite and its AP
   219	    projection.
   220	
   221	**E6. Restart.** `Event.restart v t`; order at one time: restart, ticks,
   222	deliveries. Step: `st ↦ restore (durable st)`. For an FG composite,
   223	`durable` = Λ + F + the F basis; h_max and J are derived again from
   224	received chain states. Each restart starts a new EPOCH of the node. Receipt facts (`processed`,
   225	`depsPresent`) reset with the epoch (audit W04): a block outside the
   226	durable part is lost, and it can be delivered again (the freshness check
   227	allows it). Delivery obligations toward a restarted node cover objects
   228	re-offered after its rejoin; regimes with restarts add a rejoin
   229	condition (state transfer or re-offer of the needed objects).
   230	Output monotonicity and inclusion compare reads of one node only within
   231	one epoch; a separate optional entry `catchUp` states that a rejoined
   232	node reaches its last exposed output within a bound (V11). Existing
   233	regimes add `NoRestart`.
   234	
   235	**Lift (v1 → v2).** A simulation, not only equal reads: disjoint label
   236	sum; `proposer s` ↦ seat 0 of `prop s`; `proposerOf s` = that seat's
   237	holder; old committee bijections from `CStar`'s proved equivalence (with
   238	the dependent `Fin` casts); old duty and proposal order preserved; no
   239	self-visible labels, `NoRestart`, trivial `durable`. Every v2 run in the
   240	lifted legacy regime has a corresponding v1 run. The lift transports
   241	claim PROPOSITIONS; certificate eligibility (E4, §5) is proved
   242	separately per instance. Estimate: several engineer-weeks (audit).
   243	
   244	## 3. Instances and facets (L2, L4)
   245	
   246	```lean
   247	structure Instance where
   248	  P       : ProtocolSpec
   249	  pparams : ProtocolParams      -- k, L, τ, q, Δ, …: fixed protocol identity
   250	  facets  : FacetBundle P       -- every exposed facet, once
   251	  hooks   : HooksSound P
   252	  deliv   : Deliverable P
   253	  iso     : RecordIsolated P
   254	```
   255	
   256	Protocol parameters and facets are bound once. Claim constants are NOT
   257	in the instance; they belong to certificates (§5). So one instance can
   258	hold several certified tradeoff points of one claim (for example SA's P1
   259	with (A, Π) and fast P1 with (A_f, Π); SA-PC recovery at h > a and at
   260	a ≤ f with different ρ).
   261	
   262	Facets: `ChainOutputs`, `Proposals` (labels, times, proposal identifier,
   263	`block`, `opening`), `Protected`, `Base`, `Durable`, and the FG facets
   264	of §7. Every output getter reads only `base`. Claims are schemas over
   265	output names. Phase 0 selects an extensible representation for
   266	`FacetBundle`, `FacetNeeds`, `ProtocolParams` and constant records
   267	(typed maps or separate facet records), because adding a field to a
   268	frozen Lean structure changes its declaration.
   269	
   270	## 4. Claim catalog (L3)
   271	
   272	```lean
   273	structure ClaimSpec where
   274	  id        : Lean.Name
   275	  needs     : FacetNeeds
   276	  Const     : Type                           -- this claim's constants
   277	  valid     : Const → Prop
   278	  le        : Const → Const → Bool           -- decidable; `le c c'`: c' is
   279	                                             -- at least as weak as c
   280	  le_refl   : ∀ c, le c c
   281	  le_trans  : ∀ a b c, le a b → le b c → le a c
   282	  holds     : Instance → Const → Prop
   283	  transport : ∀ I c c', valid c → valid c' → le c c' →
   284	                holds I c → holds I c'
   285	  trigger   : Instance → Const → Run → Prop  -- the claim's trigger occurs
   286	                                             -- with its deadline in the run
   287	  inhabited : Instance → Const → Prop        -- §5
   288	  refutable : RefutationKind
   289	```
   290	
   291	- Per-parameter behaviour (audit tables): the claim is weaker when θ ↑,
   292	  θrec ↑, η ↑, A ↑, A_f ↑, ρ ↑, recoveryFull false → true, G ↓, Π ↓,
   293	  faultCap ↓, and D ↑ for persistent inclusion. Exact-time inclusion is
   294	  `fixed` in D: its `le` is equality on D.
   295	- `transport` is a proof, not metadata. Composite comparisons compose
   296	  transports; `le_refl` and `le_trans` make that sound.
   297	- Relation lemmas may combine certificates of ONE instance and require
   298	  equal values for their shared constants. Example:
   299	  `Safe o ∧ included o D ⇒ includedPersist o D` (same D). `Safe o` is one
   300	  conjunction: agreement and per-node monotonicity, as today's `SafeFrom`.

