     9	B1. Review Focus 1 (line 103) and Task 6 Step 1 (line 524) test the inverse of the rule. In a contest lo = H. The rule is: a child of a block with state height < lo is eligible WITHOUT a majority (spec lines 520–523; r148 `eligible(B) := h(B.parent)<frame.lo or ...`). So a child of a height-(H−1) block is eligible (TRUE) in a contest. It is not eligible (FALSE) in the uncontested or gap frame of the same tree, where lo = H−1. The purpose of the contest is exactly this: the walk must be able to leave H−1 without a majority (N12). The fjEligible definition is correct, so the written example fails, and an implementer can "fix" the definition to match the test. Fix: in the contested view, expect fjEligible = true for a child of a height-(H−1) block (majority false, slot not current). Add the same block in an uncontested view with the expectation false.
